    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1846">

                <pb facs="1213" n="1097" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg1097"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 831.55/1.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Minister McGoodwin to the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 43.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-01-30">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 30, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith, in
                    duplicate, copies<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1846-f1"/> with translation of a
                    “Resolution “issued by the Political and Administrative Bureau of the Federal
                    District, which requires that foreigners desiring entrance to Venezuela must
                    present “certificates of good conduct” and “documents attesting their personal
                    status.”</p>
                <p>This decree, issued by order of the Acting President, is designed to prevent the
                    entry of the hordes of active Venezuelan revolutionists known to be in Trinidad,
                    Curacao, Colombia, etc., but it can be and is being invoked at will at the
                    various ports and especially along the Colombian frontier.</p>
                <p>Sir Frederic D. Harford, British Minister to Venezuela, has formally notified the
                    Venezuelan Government that his Government can not recognize the edict as
                    applying to subjects of Great Britain. While there has been no complaint filed
                    with this Legation by subjects of the United States, I would respectfully
                    request instructions.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Preston McGoodwin</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
